 \begin{abstract}
-Probing a jammed peg can reveal its misalignment and also move it beyond the corrections the robot can execute. How often does diagnosis cause this loss beyond waiting, and does accounting for it improve completion? We define recovery opportunity as the set of fixed feedback policies that achieve a declared goal within the remaining time. A matched physical probe--hold protocol measures individual-option loss and entire-repertoire erasure at a common deadline. We specify a simulation-only assembly study with geometric perturbations, native control, proprioceptive responses, and two corrective policies. H1 tests two population properties separately: at least 10\% compliant repertoire erasure under a frozen information-seeking selector, and positive predictive diagnostic signal. A secondary weighted-score test checks signal on erasure cases. H2 tests a five-percentage-point completion benefit over constrained information seeking, with matched task-aware controls. A constructed example establishes possibility, not physical prevalence. The contribution is the recovery-specific measurement and validation methodology; its contingent-planning formulation is established, and assembly validation remains prospective.
+A diagnostic contact can reveal why a manipulation failed while destroying an otherwise available correction. How frequently does this happen beyond the effect of waiting, and does anticipating it improve recovery? We study \emph{post-diagnosis recoverability}: which fixed feedback corrections still complete the task within a common deadline. Matched probe and equal-duration hold executions separate changes caused by contact from lost time, while controlled diagnostic-response tests measure information gained. We formulate two falsifiable hypotheses for contact-rich assembly. H1 predicts at least 10\% constraint-compliant loss of the entire declared recovery repertoire under a frozen information-seeking policy, together with positive diagnostic signal; a secondary test examines their co-occurrence. H2 predicts at least five percentage points higher task completion for recoverability-aware diagnosis under matched constraints and observations. We provide exact finite-model analysis, a fully specified simulation protocol, and an interpretation of positive and null outcomes. The physical hypotheses remain prospective; the contribution is a recovery-specific measurement and decision methodology, not a new contingent-planning solver.
 \end{abstract}
 
